  "query_preview": "..A weak trough over north-central Arizona this morning \nwill meander southeastward through Thursday. This will keeps the \nongoing threat for high-based and isolated convection intact, mainly \nfor the eastern zones.The trough is moving around a ridge that has \nnow been displaced southwestward, but is still close enough to \nprovide us with above normal temperatures for the next few days.\n\nBy the end of the week, the ridge moves a bit eastward, before \ncollapsing heading into the weekend as a much stronger trough enters \nthe interior Pacific Northwest. This next trough will then dig south \nor southwestward, strengthening and possibly cutting off by Sunday \nor Monday. This will likely allow for significant tropical moisture \ntransport into Arizona, along with the remnants of Hurricane Rachel. \nThe trough does not eject inland until next Tuesday or Wednesday, so \nthis moisture stream could go on for awhile.\n\nThere are still many things that could go wrong with this forecast \nand the phasing of the offshore trough and tropical moisture is not \nas dialed in as it was yesterday. Nonetheless, this is potentially \na very impactful pattern.\n\nFor now, there is higher confidence in a windy, colder and wet \npattern developing as early as Saturday and lasting well into next \nweek. There is lower confidence in QPF amounts and the potential for \nintact tropical circulation remnants actually moving into the state. \n\nStay tuned.",
